 a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38671"] :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+3)(x+1{)}^{2}(x-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-80353/selection/4e115bbc2c9d29ab867431cd42932d3e10325670ede34bd4fc551a9cd345ded3" :answer/id #uuid "064105b7-868b-5bd3-a926-9bfa7180a776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80353/selection/32b694e7aa131f284f729b2a3bea18c700b6de430843ac8a635c28a0600a96aa" :answer/id #uuid "e3190767-7e22-55a9-ba8e-3bf2e9d203b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{II}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80353/selection/500f30cea6a2bee526d42ef19673a5fa97cea6e08aabf77dedb47066f370551a" :answer/id #uuid "353d9b55-9df8-5e8a-8ba3-dd14f15616ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{III}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80353/selection/7c28bd84f9100fb35e61829bf6eff99c723771d1cd360349d8d2